==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂welh₁-|id=wool}}
From earlier {{m|en|floow|t=woolly substance, down, nap, lint}}, also spelt {{m|en|flough}}, {{m|en|flue}}, and {{m|en|flew}}, from {{der|en|vls|vluwe}},
of {{unk|en|title=uncertain}} ultimate origin:

* Compare {{cog|ang|flōh|t=that which is flown off, fragment, piece}} - see {{m|en|flaw}}
* Possibly representing a {{blend|en|flue|puff|nocap=1}}<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|fluff}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster Online|fluff}}</ref>; compare {{cog|dum|vloe}}, or perhaps {{onomatopoeic|en|title=onomatopoeic}}; compare dialectal {{cog|en|floose}}, {{m|en|flooze}}, {{m|en|fleeze|t=particles of wool or cotton; fluff; loose threads or fibres}}, {{cog|da|fnug|t=down, fluff}}, {{cog|sv|fnugg|t=speck, flake}}.
* Alternatively, {{cog|vls|vluwe}} may derive from {{der|en|fr|velu|t=hairy, furry}}, ultimately from {{der|en|la|villus||shaggy hair, tuft of hair}}.

For words of similar sound and meaning in other languages, compare {{cog|ja|フワフワ||lightly, softly|tr=fuwafuwa}}, {{cog|hu|puha}} (“soft, fluffy”), Polish {{m|pl|puchaty}} (“soft, fluffy”).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/flʌf/}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 fluff.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌf|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Anything [[light]], [[soft]] or [[fuzzy]], especially [[fur]], [[hair]], [[feather]]s.
#* {{RQ:Doyle Great Shadow|chapter=The Right of the Beacons|page=5|passage=[W]hen I walk in my fields I can see, down Berwick way, the little '''fluffs''' of white smoke which tell me of this strange new hundred-legged beast, with coals for food and a thousand men in its belly, for ever crawling over the border.}}
# Anything [[inconsequential]] or [[superficial]].
#: {{ux|en|That article was basically a bunch of '''fluff'''. It didn't say anything substantive.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[lapse]] or [[mistake]], especially a [[mistake]] in an [[actor]]'s [[line]]s.
#: {{synonyms|en|flub}}
# {{lb|en|New England}} [[marshmallow creme|Marshmallow creme]].
#: That New England-style salami and '''fluff''' sandwich sure hit the spot!
# {{lb|en|LGBT}} A [[passive]] partner in a [[lesbian]] relationship.
# {{lb|en|Australia|euphemistic}} A [[fart]]. {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|fandom slang}} [[fan fiction|Fan fiction]], or part of a fan fiction, which is [[sweet]] and [[feel-good]] in tone, usually involving [[romance]].
#* '''2011''', anonymous, quoted in Katherine Larsen & Lynn Zubernis, ''Fandom at the Crossroads: Celebration, Shame and Fan/producer Relationships'', [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Fandom_At_The_Crossroads/UC4sBwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22fluff%22+fanfic&pg=PA138 page 138]:
#*: And when something triggers, I can close the window and go read '''fluff''' for hours until I calm down.
#* '''2017''', "Flourish Kink", quoted in Ashley J. Barner, ''The Case for Fanfiction: Exploring the Pleasures and Practices of a Maligned Craft'', [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Case_for_Fanfiction/A5EwDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22fluff%22+fanfic&pg=PA67 page 67]:
#*: Fans prefer '''fluff''' to other types of fic. But angst (dramatic stories where characters have a wide range of emotions, including...angsty ones) comes in close second.
#* '''2017''', Carrie DiRisio, ''Brooding YA Hero: Becoming a Main Character (Almost) as Awesome as Me'', [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Brooding_YA_Hero/QGeCDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22fluff%22+fanfic&pg=PT347 unnumbered page]:
#*: Ah, '''fluff'''. My happy place. These fics are dedicated to feel-good feelings, which are the very best type of feelings.
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|UK|RPG}} A form of [[roleplaying]] which is inconsequential and not related to the plot; often used in the context of (but not limited to) filling time.

